Translation patch: `ParseBuf::eat_*` (24/40/48/56-bit integers)

Change: Instead of `for (i, b) in bytes.iter().enumerate() { x |= (*b as $t) << (8*i + 8*$offset) }; $t::from_le(x)` (or `from_be`), the bytes are copied to positions `$offset..$offset+SIZE` of a zero array of `size_of::<$t>()` bytes, which is decoded with `$t::from_le_bytes` (or `from_be_bytes`). The invocations name `from_le_bytes`/`from_be_bytes` instead of `from_le`/`from_be`.
Why: Avoids the `enumerate` iterator adaptor and `from_le`/`from_be` (not modelled by Aeneas); `from_le_bytes`/`from_be_bytes` are.
Behaviour: Identical. LE: both give sum of b_i << 8(i+offset). BE: `from_be(x)` = `swap_bytes(x)` on the little-endian target, giving sum of b_i << 8(N-1-i-offset), which is what `from_be_bytes` of the placed array gives (N = size of `$t`). `offset + SIZE <= N` in every invocation, so neither version panics.

